---
layout: article
title: Outputs
description: Learn the difference between HLS, DASH, and CMAF outputs in Appwrite Videos, where to find each master manifest, and which players support them.
---

An **output** is the streaming format a [rendition](/docs/products/videos/renditions) is packaged in. Appwrite Videos packages renditions as **HLS**, **DASH**, or **CMAF**. Each output has a **master manifest**: the playlist a player loads first, which lists every rendition and caption track so the player can pick the best one.

# What a manifest looks like {% #what-a-manifest-looks-like %}

A manifest is a small text file. You rarely read one, but seeing it makes the rest of this page easier to follow. A simplified HLS master manifest for a three-rung ladder looks like this:

```text
#EXTM3U
#EXT-X-STREAM-INF:BANDWIDTH=5192000,RESOLUTION=1920x1080
renditions/<RENDITION_ID_1080P>/streams/0/playlist.m3u8
#EXT-X-STREAM-INF:BANDWIDTH=2928000,RESOLUTION=1280x720
renditions/<RENDITION_ID_720P>/streams/0/playlist.m3u8
#EXT-X-STREAM-INF:BANDWIDTH=1528000,RESOLUTION=854x480
renditions/<RENDITION_ID_480P>/streams/0/playlist.m3u8
```

Each entry tells the player how much bandwidth a rendition needs and where its playlist lives. The player measures its own connection and picks the best entry it can sustain. A DASH `.mpd` manifest carries the same information as XML.

# Choose an output {% #choose-an-output %}

| Output | Segments | Master manifests | Plays on |
|--------|----------|------------------|----------|
| **HLS** | MPEG-TS | `master.m3u8` | Safari, iOS, and tvOS natively. hls.js everywhere else |
| **DASH** | fMP4 | `master.mpd` | dash.js, Shaka Player, ExoPlayer, and most smart TVs. Not native in Safari |
| **CMAF** | fMP4 | `master.m3u8` and `master.mpd` | Everything HLS and DASH play on |

**CMAF** is the default in the Console. It encodes fMP4 segments once and exposes both an HLS and a DASH master manifest for them, so you get both formats for the encoding time and storage of one.

Choose **HLS** only when a player or device you support expects MPEG-TS segments. Choose **DASH** only when you never need HLS, for example an Android-only app on ExoPlayer.

If you are not sure, choose CMAF. You can add another output later by creating renditions for it, without touching the ones you have.

# Master manifest URLs {% #master-manifest-urls %}

Hand the master manifest URL to your player. Players follow it to the right rendition, segments, and captions on their own.

| Output | URL |
|--------|-----|
| HLS | `/v1/videos/<VIDEO_ID>/outputs/hls/master.m3u8` |
| DASH | `/v1/videos/<VIDEO_ID>/outputs/dash/master.mpd` |
| CMAF (HLS) | `/v1/videos/<VIDEO_ID>/outputs/cmaf/master.m3u8` |
| CMAF (DASH) | `/v1/videos/<VIDEO_ID>/outputs/cmaf/master.mpd` |

Add your endpoint in front and your project ID as a query parameter:

```text
https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/videos/<VIDEO_ID>/outputs/cmaf/master.m3u8?project=<PROJECT_ID>
```

In the Console, open a video and select **Streaming** to copy every URL, including the per-rendition playlists and caption manifests. The SDKs build the same URLs with `getHlsManifest()`, `getDashManifest()`, `getCmafHlsManifest()`, and `getCmafDashManifest()`.

A master manifest lists only renditions that are `ready`. Until one is ready, there is nothing to stream. When more renditions finish, the manifest grows, and players pick them up the next time they load it.

The URL is stable. It does not change when you add or delete renditions, so you can store it, or rebuild it from the video ID whenever you need it.

# What a player requests {% #what-a-player-requests %}

1. The **master manifest**, which lists each rendition with its resolution and bitrate, and each caption track.
2. A **media playlist** for the rendition it picks (HLS and CMAF HLS). DASH lists segments in the master manifest itself.
3. **Segments**, a few seconds of video each, one after another. The player moves up or down the ladder between segments as bandwidth changes.

All of these requests use the read permissions of the source file. See [Permissions](/docs/products/videos/permissions).

Players follow these links on their own. You only need the URLs below when you debug a stream or build tooling around it:

| Resource | SDK method |
|----------|------------|
| Media playlist for one rendition stream (HLS) | `getStreamManifest({ videoId, renditionId, streamId })` |
| Media playlist for one rendition stream (CMAF HLS) | `getCmafStreamManifest({ videoId, renditionId, streamId })` |
| Caption manifest for an output | `getCaptionManifest({ videoId, captionId, output })` |

`streamId` is the position of a track inside the rendition, starting at `0`. HLS segments are served as `video/mp2t`, and DASH and CMAF segments as `video/iso.segment`.

# Pick a manifest at runtime {% #pick-a-manifest-at-runtime %}

With CMAF, one video has both an HLS and a DASH manifest. On the web you can choose per browser: Safari plays HLS natively, and other browsers play either format through a library.

```js
const base = `https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/videos/${videoId}/outputs/cmaf`;
const query = '?project=<PROJECT_ID>';

const video = document.createElement('video');
const nativeHls = video.canPlayType('application/vnd.apple.mpegurl') !== '';

const src = nativeHls
    ? `${base}/master.m3u8${query}` // Safari, iOS: play directly
    : `${base}/master.mpd${query}`;  // Elsewhere: load with Shaka Player or dash.js
```

Most apps do not need this. A single HLS player such as hls.js, with the native fallback shown in [Play a video in your app](/docs/products/videos/play), covers every modern browser.

# Player support {% #player-support %}

| Player | Platform | HLS | DASH |
|--------|----------|-----|------|
| hls.js | Web | Yes | No |
| Shaka Player | Web | Yes | Yes |
| Video.js | Web | Yes | Yes |
| Native `<video>` | Safari | Yes | No |
| AVPlayer | Apple platforms | Yes | No |
| Media3 ExoPlayer | Android | Yes | Yes |
| video_player | Flutter | Yes | No |
| react-native-video | React Native | Yes | No |

For CMAF, use the HLS manifest with HLS players and the DASH manifest with DASH players. See [Play a video in your app](/docs/products/videos/play) for setup code.
